The '''Sarta Colony of Guajicutea (1683–1705)''' covers the period of the [[Sarta-Volisania War]] and its impact on [[Guajicutea]]. While the war was fought primarily in the {{w|Old World}} between [[Sarta]] and [[Volisania]], the colony was transformed from a stable colonial possession into an isolated fortress, suffered economic collapse, experienced a peasant revolt that overthrew and killed the governor, and underwent a forced transition away from the colonial plantation system. The war ended in 1705 with Sarta bankrupted and landlocked, its fleets destroyed, retaining its colonial possession only through the intervention of [[Breisland]] and [[Mercia]].

The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] was ceded to the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]], a Volisanian royal trading company, under the terms of the Treaty of 1705. The colony's primary industrial asset passed from Sartan to Volisanian control.

== War begins (1683) ==

The [[Sarta-Volisania War]] began in 1683. The conflict was fought primarily in the Old World between the two powers; in the {{w|New World}}, the impact on Guajicutea was economic and political rather than military.

Upon the outbreak of war, the colony was put on a war footing. The [[Castillo de San Esteban]] was fully garrisoned, the harbor at [[Waribá]] was fortified, and the Sartan naval squadron stationed at Waribá was ordered to remain in port for defensive purposes. This squadron would prove to be the only Sartan fleet to survive the war.

Volisanian trade ended immediately. The trade relationship that had matured through the shared [[Petran Catholic]] tradition was severed overnight. Volisanian merchants departed Waribá, and the colony lost its nearest trade partner in the western Capuyaquiran.

== War years and economic crisis (1683–1690) ==

=== Isolation ===

As the war progressed, the colony became increasingly isolated from the Sartan mainland. Communications were disrupted by Volisanian naval operations. Sartan funding for the colonial administration — which had paid officials, military, and infrastructure — dried up as the war drained the mainland treasury. By the late 1680s, the colony was effectively self-funding, and the colonial government lacked the resources to maintain the administrative and military apparatus established over the preceding decades.

=== Shipbuilding ===

The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] shifted to war production, building galleons for the Sartan war effort. The shipyard's hybrid Sartan-Guaji construction techniques — Illypnian hull forms with [[Yabikú]] joinery and local tropical hardwoods — produced durable vessels suited to long ocean voyages. However, as Sarta's financial situation deteriorated, the crown could no longer pay for the ships under construction. By the early 1690s, shipbuilding at the Astillero Real had trickled to a halt. Unfinished hulls sat in the dry docks, and shipwrights — both Sartan and Guaji — went unpaid.

=== Trade ===

With Volisanian trade severed, the colony survived on trade with neutral parties. [[Mercia]], [[Breisland]], and [[Burawa]] — all neutral in the Sarta-Volisania conflict — maintained trade relationships with the colony through their respective colonial possessions. Mercian and Breislandic ships called regularly at Waribá, and Burawi merchants — present in Waribá since the 1634 normalization — continued to trade.

The colony also accepted dockage of foreign navy vessels. Mercian, Breislandic, and Burawi naval ships used Waribá as a resupply point, taking advantage of the colony's harbor facilities and the [[Castillo de San Esteban]]'s protective guns. The presence of foreign warships provided a degree of security that the colony's own diminished garrison could not.

=== Economic collapse ===

The loss of Sartan mainland funding produced a cascading economic crisis. Colonial officials and military personnel went unpaid. The plantation system, which had relied on Sartan capital and export markets, began to break down. In rural areas, a barter economy emerged as colonial currency lost value.

Food shortages worsened as supply lines were disrupted and the island's population — approximately 80,000 — strained agricultural capacity. The deforestation and soil erosion that had begun in the embargo era continued, reducing yields on cleared slopes.

=== Harsher laws and taxation ===

As the colonial administration lost access to Sartan funding, it turned to extraction from the local population to sustain the war effort and the colonial government.

Governor Ortega declared martial law in 1685, granting himself emergency powers over the colonial judiciary, movement, and labor. Curfews were imposed in [[Waribá]] and [[Tabakurí]]. Movement between districts was restricted, requiring official passes. All foreign trade was required to pass through official channels, and private commerce with Volisania — already the enemy — was criminalized.

A war tax was levied on top of existing colonial taxes. A separate shipbuilding levy required communities to contribute timber, resin, fiber cordage, and labor to the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]]. Tax collectors became increasingly aggressive as revenues fell — seizure of property for non-payment became common, and imprisonment for tax evasion was enforced. The tax burden fell heaviest on Guaji and Bayani peasants, while Sartan colonial elites and landowners were largely exempt or could evade payment.

Forced labor conscription was introduced for the Astillero Real. Men were pulled from agricultural work into the shipyard to meet galleon production quotas. This removed labor from food production at precisely the moment when agricultural capacity was already strained, worsening the food crisis.

Food requisitioning was instituted in 1687. Colonial authorities seized crops and livestock from rural communities "for the war effort" and garrison supply, paying below-market rates or nothing. Communities that resisted requisition faced military punishment.

By the late 1680s, the colonial administration was extracting from a population that had less and less to give. Each round of extraction produced more resistance, which required more enforcement, which required more resources — a spiral that ended with Ortega's death in 1691.

=== Conditions on the ground ===

The war years were devastating for the civilian population. The combination of food shortages, forced labor, taxation, and the breakdown of colonial services produced a humanitarian crisis that worsened throughout the late 1680s.

Order broke down as deserters from the unpaid colonial military turned to banditry on the roads between settlements. Rural communities formed self-defense militias, further eroding the colonial administration's monopoly on force. Travel between [[Tabakurí]] and [[Waribá]] became dangerous without armed escort. Internal displacement accelerated in both directions: rural populations fled to Waribá seeking food and the protection of foreign naval vessels in the port, while others fled the cities to escape taxation and violence, retreating to subsistence farming.

Disease followed malnutrition. Outbreaks of fever and dysentery struck the weakened population, particularly in the crowded conditions of Waribá. The colonial medical system could not cope, and the [[Petran Catholic|Sartan Petran Church]]'s charitable institutions were themselves depleted. The church was caught between the colonial administration, which demanded support for the war effort, and the starving population. Some priests distributed church food stores to peasants in defiance of the governor's orders; others aligned with the administration, preaching obedience. The church fractured along the same lines as the rest of colonial society.

With men conscripted into the shipyard and military, women carried agricultural production in many communities, gaining economic authority that would persist after the war. The [[Kingdom of Murani]] connection became a survival lifeline: cross-strait smuggling, always informal, became essential for food, medicine, and information. Murani fishermen and traders brought supplies that the official trade network could not provide. Military desertion accelerated throughout the late 1680s as unpaid soldiers left their posts — some joining peasant communities, others turning to banditry. The garrison at the [[Castillo de San Esteban]] shrank, reducing the administration's ability to enforce order.

== Peasant revolts and the collapse of the plantation system ==

The economic crisis produced multiple peasant revolts throughout the late 1680s and early 1690s. These revolts were driven by food shortages, increased taxation, forced labor, and the breakdown of the colonial economic system. Guaji and Bayani peasants, who had worked the plantation estates under Sartan landowners and administrators, revolted against the colonial land-tenure system.

The [[Waribá Food Riots]] (1686) were the first major outbreak. Food prices in the port city had doubled as supply lines disrupted and requisitioning diverted crops to the garrison. Dock workers, fishermen, and Bayani laborers rioted in the market district, seizing food stores and clashing with colonial police. The riots were suppressed, but they marked the beginning of sustained unrest.

The [[Southern Peasant Revolt]] (1688–1689) was larger and more organized. Plantation workers in the southern agricultural districts — the center of the cash crop economy — refused to deliver harvests to colonial authorities, seized plantation estates, and expelled Sartan landowners. The revolt spread across multiple plantations and required military force to suppress. Some seized lands were never returned to their colonial owners.

The [[Tabakurí Uprising]] (1690) brought the unrest to the inland capital. Peasants from the central river valleys marched on Tabakurí, protesting taxation and food requisitioning. The garrison at Tabakurí dispersed the marchers, but the uprising demonstrated that unrest had spread from the ports and plantations to the interior.

Each revolt weakened the governor's authority further. The colonial administration could suppress individual outbreaks but could not address the underlying causes — food shortage, taxation, and the collapse of the colonial economic system. Each suppression required military resources the administration could not afford, and each outbreak recruited more people to the cause.

The revolts forced a transition away from the plantation-feudal system. Estates were abandoned or seized by peasant communities. The colonial land-tenure system, which had concentrated land in the hands of Sartan administrators and landowners, gave way to smallholder farming as peasants reclaimed land for subsistence agriculture. By the end of the war period, the plantation system that had characterized the colony since the early 1600s had largely collapsed, replaced by a patchwork of small farms and communal land use.

== The Interregnum of 1691 ==

In 1691, the crisis reached its breaking point. Governor [[Felipe Ortega]] — who had governed since 1674, the longest-serving governor in the colony's history — was overthrown and killed by a peasant mob in [[Waribá]]. The mob, driven by food shortages and increased taxation, stormed the governor's residence. Ortega's death marked the collapse of colonial authority on the island.

For three months in 1691, the colony was effectively ungoverned. A peasant ruler known as [[Waraku]] — a Guaji name meaning "Turning Moon," evoking the lunar calendar and the old ways — seized power in Waribá. Waraku was a Guaji peasant who had emerged as a leader during the revolts. His rule was brief, chaotic, and largely confined to the Waribá area; the rest of the island was in varying states of disorder, with local communities governing themselves and the remaining colonial military fighting to restore order.

The period known as the [[Interregnum of 1691]] — or locally, Waraku's Three Months — was marked by violence, food riots, and the breakdown of colonial institutions. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] was idle. The [[Castillo de San Esteban]] garrison, unpaid and divided, could not project authority beyond the citadel walls.

=== Burawi retaking attempt ===

Word of the colony's collapse reached the Burawi colonial administration in [[Lacashe]]. Burawa, which had lost Guajicutea to Sarta in 1609, saw an opportunity to retake the island. A fleet of Burawi ships was dispatched from Lacashe to Guajicutea.

The Burawi fleet arrived at Waribá to find the colony in chaos. The port was disorderly, the colonial government nonexistent, and the local population hostile to any external authority. The Burawi force was unable to establish a beachhead — not because of organized resistance, but because the chaos itself prevented any party from establishing control. Skirmishes with both peasant militias and remnants of the colonial garrison convinced the Burawi commander that retaking the island would require a force and resources that the Lacashe administration was unwilling to commit. The fleet withdrew.

=== Restoration (1692) ===

In 1692, an alliance of [[Breisland]] and [[Mercia]] — both concerned about the power vacuum in the Capuyaquiran — dispatched ships to Guajicutea to restore order. The Breislandic-Mercian fleet arrived at Waribá, secured the port, and established a temporary administration.

A Sartan ship captain, [[Andrés Caldera]], was installed as temporary administrator. Caldera was a naval officer serving with the Waribá squadron — one of the few Sartan authority figures remaining on the island. His appointment was arranged through diplomatic channels between Sarta, Breisland, and Mercia. Caldera was not a crown-appointed governor but a military officer acting as interim civil administrator, governing with the support of the Breislandic and Mercian naval presence.

== Temporary administration (1692–1705) ==

Caldera's administration governed the colony for thirteen years, from 1692 to the end of the war in 1705. The period was one of continued economic hardship but relative stability. Caldera maintained order with a small force of colonial military and the implicit backing of the Breislandic and Mercian naval presence at Waribá.

The colonial economy remained depressed. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] operated at minimal capacity — enough to maintain and repair the Sartan naval squadron but without the resources for new construction. Trade with Mercia, Breisland, and Burawa sustained the colony at a subsistence level. The plantation system did not recover; the smallholder agriculture that had emerged during the peasant revolts became the dominant pattern.

The colonial administration was a shadow of its pre-war self. Caldera governed with a reduced bureaucracy and relied on local community leaders — Guaji village heads, Bayani community elders, and the remaining Sartan colonial personnel — to maintain order in the interior. The [[Castillo de San Esteban]] garrison remained the colony's military anchor, but its strength had diminished through desertion and lack of pay.

== Treaty of 1705 ==

The [[Sarta-Volisania War]] ended in 1705. The war had been devastating for Sarta: the mainland was bankrupted, its fleets destroyed on the high seas, and its coastal territories lost. Sarta was left landlocked — a continental power with no navy and no sea access, retaining only a colonial possession in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} that it could barely reach.

The Sartan naval squadron at Waribá — the only fleet to survive the war, having been ordered to remain in port throughout the conflict — was the sole remnant of Sartan naval power.

The Treaty of 1705 confirmed Guajicutea as a Sartan colonial possession. However, the treaty forced Sarta to cede the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] — the colony's shipyard and dry docks — to the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]], a Volisanian royal trading company. The colony's primary industrial asset, and its capacity for naval construction and repair, passed from Sartan to Volisanian control.

The cession of the Astillero Real was a strategic blow. The shipyard had been the colony's industrial heart and a major employer. Under Volisanian ownership, the shipyard's output would serve Volisanian commercial interests, not Sartan military needs. The Sartan naval squadron at Waribá would henceforth depend on a Volisanian company for its repair and maintenance.

== Aftermath ==

The war left the colony fundamentally changed. The plantation-feudal system had collapsed, replaced by smallholder agriculture. The colonial administration was diminished and dependent on foreign support. The colony's industrial capacity was in Volisanian hands. Sarta, landlocked and bankrupt, could provide little support to its distant colony.

The population, which had reached approximately 80,000 before the war, had declined during the crisis years through famine, violence, and emigration. The colony that emerged from the war was poorer, more autonomous, and more Guaji in character than it had been before. The cultural fusion of the 1630–1683 period survived, but the colonial hierarchy that had sustained it was broken.

